\description{
Takes two independent samples of scores that can be ordered and returns
Vargha and Delaney's (2000) \emph{A}, the probability that a randomly
drawn observation from the first population exceeds a randomly drawn
observation from the second (a tie counting one half), with its standard
error, a confidence interval, and a test of \eqn{A = 1/2}. The statistic
uses only the order of the observations, so it needs no assumption of
normality or of equal variances and suits ordinal and skewed outcomes.
}
\details{
\strong{Estimand and interval.} For independent observations \eqn{X}
from the first population and \eqn{Y} from the second,
\deqn{A = \Pr(X > Y) + \tfrac{1}{2}\,\Pr(X = Y).}
The sampling units are the \eqn{n_1 + n_2} individuals, each observed
once and each in one group. The interval is a large-sample (Wald)
confidence interval for the population \emph{A}, formed on the logit
scale by default. A value of \eqn{A = 0.5} is stochastic equality, and
\eqn{A > 0.5} says that the first group tends to score higher.
Stochastic equality (Vargha & Delaney, 2000, p. 102) is weaker than
equality of the two distributions: populations that differ in spread or
shape can have \eqn{A = 0.5}. The test reported here is of
\eqn{A = 0.5}. It is not a test that the distributions are identical,
the null hypothesis of the Wilcoxon-Mann-Whitney test (pp. 108--109).
Qualitative magnitude labels for \emph{A} are not reported, in keeping
with the DMAR convention of reporting effect sizes as numbers with
confidence intervals.

\strong{Assumptions.} The two samples are independent of each other and
the observations within each are independent and identically
distributed; for paired observations use
\code{\link{probability_of_superiority_paired}}. The response is at
least ordinal. The estimate needs nothing more: no distributional
shape, no equality of variances, and ties are allowed. The standard
error, the interval, and the \emph{t} and \emph{z} tests are
large-sample results; \emph{The test} and \emph{When the estimated
variance is zero} say how they behave in small samples.

\strong{Scale and missing values.} \emph{A} depends only on the order of
the observations (Vargha & Delaney, 2000, pp. 101, 107), so any
increasing rescoring of the response gives the same value, and an
ordinal response may be supplied as an ordered factor. Missing values
(\code{NA} and \code{NaN}) are removed group by group; the numbers
removed are in the \code{n_missing} attribute. Infinite values are
ordered as usual. Dates and date-times (classes \code{Date} and
\code{POSIXct}) are ordered by their numeric values, as are two time
differences (class \code{difftime}) in the same units. A one-column
matrix and a one-dimensional array are read as the vectors they are.
Each group needs at least two non-missing observations; with fewer the
function stops, since no variance can be estimated.

\strong{What is refused, and why.} The statistic uses the order of the
observations and nothing else, so the function must be given objects
whose order is the order meant. It refuses, with a condition of class
\code{dmar_scores_not_ordered} (see \code{\link{dmar_metadata}}), an
unordered factor (its codes follow the order of \code{levels()},
alphabetical unless set, so the levels low, mid, high are stored as 2,
3, 1, and \emph{A} computed from the codes of the example in
\emph{Examples} is 0.406 where the ordered levels give 0.656); a
character vector (strings sort alphabetically, \code{"10"} before
\code{"9"}); an ordered factor beside anything that is not an ordered
factor with the same levels in the same order (each factor numbers its
own levels from 1); time differences in different units, or one beside
plain numbers (each such object is stored in its own units); a
\code{Date} beside a \code{POSIXct}; and a list, data frame, or matrix
of several columns. Nothing is computed in any of these cases. The
message says which case applies and what to supply: an ordered factor
with its levels from lowest to highest,
\code{factor(x, levels = c("low", "mid", "high"), ordered = TRUE)}, or
numeric scores.

\strong{Sample estimate.} With \eqn{\bar R_X} the mean rank of the
first sample in the pooled ranking, mid-ranks for ties,
\deqn{\hat A = \frac{\bar R_X - (n_1 + 1)/2}{n_2}}
(Vargha & Delaney, 2000, Equation 14, p. 107). Equivalently,
\eqn{\hat A = U / (n_1 n_2)}, with \eqn{U} the Mann-Whitney statistic
that counts the pairs with \eqn{X_i > Y_j} and half of the tied pairs.
The function computes the mean rank form, whose rounding can differ
from the exact ratio \eqn{U/(n_1 n_2)} in about the thirteenth decimal
place.

\strong{Standard error.} Define the placements
\deqn{V_{10}(X_i) = \frac{1}{n_2}\sum_{j} \psi(X_i, Y_j), \qquad
        V_{01}(Y_j) = \frac{1}{n_1}\sum_{i} \psi(X_i, Y_j),}
with \eqn{\psi(x, y) = 1, \tfrac{1}{2}, 0} as \eqn{x > y, =, <}. The
variance estimate is
\deqn{\widehat{\mathrm{Var}}(\hat A) = \frac{S^2_{10}}{n_1} +
                                       \frac{S^2_{01}}{n_2},}
where \eqn{S^2_{10}} and \eqn{S^2_{01}} are the sample variances
(divisors \eqn{n_1 - 1} and \eqn{n_2 - 1}) of the \eqn{V_{10}} and
\eqn{V_{01}} placements. This is the placement (structural components)
estimator of DeLong, DeLong, and Clarke-Pearson (1988, p. 840) for the
area under a single receiver operating characteristic curve;
Schüürhuis, Konietschke, and Brunner (2025) state that the variance
estimator of Brunner and Munzel (2000) is identical to it. It is
consistent for two independent samples from any pair of distributions,
with no parametric or equal-variance assumption. It is not unbiased in
general: its expectation exceeds the variance of \eqn{\hat A} by
\eqn{(\xi_{11} - \xi_{10} - \xi_{01})/(n_1 n_2) \ge 0}, where
\eqn{\xi_{11}} is the variance of \eqn{\psi(X, Y)} and \eqn{\xi_{10}}
and \eqn{\xi_{01}} are the covariances of two values of \eqn{\psi} that
share their \eqn{X} or their \eqn{Y}. The excess is zero when both
variables take only two values. With three continuous observations per
group from one distribution the variance is 7/108 and the estimator
averages 8/108.

\strong{Confidence interval.} \code{ci_method = "logit"} (the default)
forms a Wald interval for \eqn{\mathrm{logit}(A) = \log\{A/(1-A)\}}
with the delta method standard error
\eqn{\widehat{\mathrm{SE}}(\hat A)/\{\hat A(1 - \hat A)\}},
\deqn{\mathrm{logit}(\hat A) \pm z_{1 - \alpha/2}\,
  \frac{\widehat{\mathrm{SE}}(\hat A)}{\hat A(1 - \hat A)},}
and transforms the two limits back with the inverse logit, so they stay
inside (0, 1). Pohar Perme and Manevski (2019) conclude that the DeLong
variance estimator is reliable and that the interval should be formed
on the logit scale. \code{ci_method = "wald"} returns
\eqn{\hat A \pm z_{1 - \alpha/2}\,\widehat{\mathrm{SE}}(\hat A)},
clipped to \eqn{[0, 1]}. Both intervals use the normal quantile,
whatever \code{test} is.

\strong{Relation to Cliff's delta.} \eqn{A = (\delta + 1)/2}, and
because \eqn{\mathrm{arctanh}(2A - 1) = \mathrm{logit}(A)/2}, the logit
interval here and the interval of \code{\link{cliff_delta}} are the
same construction on the two scales. The two functions estimate the
variance differently, however (placement variances here; there the
sample version of Feng and Cliff's, 2004, Equation 2, which has a third
term), so their limits are not the same after the change of scale. For
\code{x = 1:5} and \code{y = 3:7}, \eqn{\hat A = 0.18} with logit limits
0.034 and 0.576, which are \eqn{-0.931} and 0.151 on the scale of
\eqn{\delta}; \code{cliff_delta()} gives \eqn{\hat\delta = -0.64} with
limits \eqn{-0.935} and 0.177.

\strong{The test.} The statistic is
\deqn{W = \frac{\hat A - 1/2}{\widehat{\mathrm{SE}}(\hat A)},}
reported in \code{test_statistic}. The three choices of \code{test}
share this statistic and differ in what it is referred to.
\code{"t"} gives \eqn{p = 2\,\Pr(T_f \ge |W|)} for Student's \emph{t}
with
\deqn{f = \frac{\left(S^2_{10}/n_1 + S^2_{01}/n_2\right)^2}
  {\dfrac{(S^2_{10}/n_1)^2}{n_1 - 1} + \dfrac{(S^2_{01}/n_2)^2}{n_2 - 1}}}
degrees of freedom, reported in \code{df}. The statistic, the degrees
of freedom, and the rejection rule are those of Schüürhuis,
Konietschke, and Brunner (2025, Equations 3 and 7, the display that
precedes Equation 8, and Equation 8), written here in placement
variances; they present them as the test of Brunner and Munzel (2000),
whose statistic is their Equation 4.8 (p. 21) and whose degrees of
freedom are their Equation 5.9 (p. 22), an approximation they recommend
for group sizes of at least 10.
\code{"z"} gives \eqn{p = 2\,\Phi(-|W|)}.
\code{"permutation"} recomputes \eqn{W} for reassignments of the
\eqn{n_1 + n_2} pooled scores to groups of sizes \eqn{n_1} and
\eqn{n_2}: every one of the \code{choose(n_1 + n_2, n_1)} reassignments
when there are at most 50,000 (or when \code{exact = TRUE}), and
\code{n_resamples} randomly drawn ones otherwise, by the rules of
\code{\link{randomization_test}}. The \emph{p}-value is the share of
reassignments with \eqn{|W_b| \ge |W|} under enumeration, and
\eqn{(1 + r)/(1 + m)} with \eqn{r} such reassignments among \eqn{m}
random ones. A reassignment whose estimated variance is 0 has
\eqn{W_b = 0} when its \eqn{\hat A} is 1/2 and \eqn{W_b} infinite, with
the sign of \eqn{\hat A - 1/2}, otherwise. Neubert and Brunner (2007, p. 5195) define only the
one-sided \emph{p}-value of their studentized permutation test, the
share of reassignments whose statistic is at least the observed one,
with the variance recomputed for each. Schüürhuis et al. describe
its two-sided \emph{p}-value as twice the smaller tail share; the form
used here, the share at least as extreme in absolute value, is the one
\code{randomization_test()} uses, and the two can differ. The
permutation test is exact when the two distributions are equal, since
every reassignment is then equally likely, and approximate when they
differ in spread or shape with \eqn{A = 1/2}.

The \emph{z} reference rejects a true \eqn{A = 1/2} too often in small
samples, which is why it is not the default. Rejection rates at
a nominal .05 with normal data and \eqn{A = 1/2}, from this function
(10,000 samples per row for \emph{z} and \emph{t}, Monte Carlo standard
error about .002; 2,000 samples for the permutation test, standard
error about .005, with 399 random reassignments where the
reassignments were not enumerated); the ratio is the standard deviation
of the first group over that of the second:
\tabular{rrrrrr}{
  \eqn{n_1} \tab \eqn{n_2} \tab SD ratio \tab \emph{z} \tab \emph{t} \tab permutation \cr
  5 \tab 5 \tab 1 \tab .086 \tab .071 \tab .047 \cr
  10 \tab 10 \tab 1 \tab .073 \tab .056 \tab .047 \cr
  8 \tab 20 \tab 1 \tab .080 \tab .062 \tab .045 \cr
  20 \tab 20 \tab 1 \tab .060 \tab .052 \tab .046 \cr
  5 \tab 5 \tab 3 \tab .084 \tab .068 \tab .048 \cr
  10 \tab 10 \tab 3 \tab .079 \tab .051 \tab .052 \cr
  8 \tab 20 \tab 3 \tab .090 \tab .062 \tab .063 \cr
  20 \tab 8 \tab 3 \tab .065 \tab .052 \tab .034 \cr
  20 \tab 20 \tab 3 \tab .062 \tab .050 \tab .052
}
Over these and the other simulations run for this function, which
include five-category ordinal and lognormal data and group sizes from 5
to 30, the rates were .056 to .099 for \emph{z}, .049 to .071 for
\emph{t}, and .032 to .063 for the permutation test. The \emph{t}
reference removes most of the excess from 10 per group upward and part
of it below that. These ranges describe the conditions that were run.
With one group of 5 or fewer and the other five or more times larger,
the \emph{t} reference rejected a true \eqn{A = 1/2} in .07 to .11 of
samples when the two distributions were equal (.073 with 5 and 25, .085
with 4 and 20, .112 with 3 and 30; normal data, 20,000 samples each),
where the permutation test is exact (the \emph{z} reference rejected in
.105, .124, and .172 of the same samples). When the smaller group has
fewer than about 6 scores, \code{test = "permutation"} is therefore the
test to use, unless the two groups also differ greatly in spread, the
case described next. When the small group also had five
or six times the standard deviation of the large one, so that the
distributions were not equal, the permutation test rejected in .08 of
samples with 5 and 25 and in .11 with 4 and 20 (4,000 samples each, 999
reassignments). When the groups are completely separated the
estimated variance is 0 and only the permutation test gives a
\emph{p}-value: \eqn{W} is infinite, and the \emph{p}-value is the share
of reassignments that are also completely separated (2/252 for 5 and 5
untied scores).

The test and the default interval are separate procedures. The interval
is formed on the logit scale so that it stays inside 0 and 1, and the
test is on the scale of \emph{A}. They can disagree in small samples:
for \code{x = c(3.5, 5, 6, 7, 8)} and \code{y = c(1, 2, 3, 4, 4.2)},
\eqn{\hat A = .92}, the logit interval is [.486, .993], the \emph{t}
test gives \emph{p} = .0033, and the exact permutation test gives
\emph{p} = .032. A test on the logit scale would agree with the
interval but rejects far too rarely (.007 to .041 at a nominal .05 in
simulation) and is not offered.

\strong{When the estimated variance is zero.} Two kinds of data give a
variance estimate of exactly 0, and they are the only two.
\emph{Complete separation}: every observation in one group exceeds
every observation in the other, so the estimate is at the end of its
range (\eqn{\hat A} 0 or 1). \emph{No variation}: every observation has
the same value, so the estimate is 0.5. In both, the two limits are
reported equal to the estimate under either \code{ci_method},
\code{se} is 0, \code{test_statistic} and \code{p_value} are \code{NA}
(under \code{test = "permutation"} the test is reported, as described
above),
the table's \code{interval_degenerate} attribute names the case, and a
warning of class \code{dmar_interval_degenerate} is given whose
\code{reason} element is \code{"complete_separation"} or
\code{"no_variation"}; the condition also carries \code{estimate},
\code{n} (the two group sizes), and \code{limit_feng_cliff}. The
reported limits are not a confidence interval with the stated coverage.
The zero describes the sample: the variance estimate measures how much
the comparisons differ from one observation to the next, and without
overlap they do not differ. Complete separation is common when the
effect is large and the samples are small. With five observations per
group from normal populations, it occurred in 19 percent of samples
when the population value of \emph{A} was 0.86 and in 59 percent when
it was 0.96, and across all samples the reported 95 percent limits then
covered the population value 79 and about 40 percent of the time, against 98
and 96 percent among the samples that were not separated (20,000
simulated samples each). Feng and Cliff (2004, p. 324, Equations 6 to
8) treat this case by assuming the largest variance \eqn{\hat\delta} can
have, which gives the limit \eqn{(n_b - z^2)/(n_b + z^2)} when
\eqn{\hat\delta = 1} (and its negative when \eqn{\hat\delta = -1}), with
\eqn{n_b} the smaller group size; they describe the result as
conservative. On the scale of \emph{A} that limit is
\eqn{n_b/(n_b + z^2)} when \eqn{\hat A = 1} and \eqn{z^2/(n_b + z^2)}
when \eqn{\hat A = 0}: 0.566 and 0.434 at the 95 percent level with
five in the smaller group. The warning states that limit. It is not the
limit this function reports.

Particular cases. Infinite values are ordered like any others, so a
group made up of \code{Inf} beside finite scores is completely
separated, and two groups made up of the same infinite value have no
variation. Two constant groups at different values are completely
separated. Groups that only touch, the largest score of one equal to
the smallest of the other, are not separated: the tied pair gives a
positive variance and an ordinary interval, which is wide
(\code{c(3, 4, 5)} against \code{c(1, 2, 3)} gives \eqn{\hat A = 0.944}
with limits 0.475 and 0.997). The mark travels with the result:
\code{\link{results_sentence}} writes the estimate and says that there
is no confidence interval, the printed table says so beneath itself,
\code{tidy()} and \code{glance()} carry it in the column
\code{interval_degenerate}, \code{\link{randomization_test}} passes on
the warning of \code{\link{cliff_delta}}, and
\code{\link{ss_aipe_cliff_delta_sensitivity}} counts a separated
replication as one without an interval. The randomization test remains
valid in both cases.

\strong{Relation to other measures.} \emph{A} is the area under the
receiver operating characteristic curve (AUC) when the scores are used
to tell the two groups apart, and \eqn{\hat A = U/(n_1 n_2)} for the
Mann-Whitney \emph{U}. Cliff's \eqn{\delta} is \eqn{2A - 1}. The
common language effect size of McGraw and Wong (1992), as
\code{\link{cles}} computes it, is \eqn{\Phi(\delta/\sqrt{2})} with
\eqn{\delta} the standardized mean difference, which is
\eqn{\Pr(X > Y)} for normal distributions with a common variance;
\emph{A} estimates that probability (with ties split) without those
assumptions.

\strong{Reading the output.} In the second example below the first
level of \code{treatment}, Control, is group 1, with 246 children
against 64. \eqn{\hat A = 0.429}: a randomly drawn control child has
the higher \code{iq_8} than a randomly drawn child from the bloomer
group with estimated probability .43, ties counting one half. The 95
percent logit interval is [0.351, 0.511], and the \emph{t} test of
\eqn{A = 1/2} has \eqn{W = -1.72} on 92.5 degrees of freedom,
\emph{p} = .089.
}
\examples{
# Two numeric vectors.
set.seed(113)
x <- rnorm(40, mean = 0.6)
y <- rnorm(40, mean = 0)
vargha_delaney_A(x, y)

# Formula interface on the pygmalion field experiment. The first factor
# level (Control) forms group 1, so A below 0.5 says a randomly drawn
# control child tends to score below a child from the bloomer group.
vargha_delaney_A(iq_8 ~ treatment, data = pygmalion)

# Wald (untransformed) interval rather than logit.
vargha_delaney_A(x, y, ci_method = "wald")

# The studentized permutation test and the normal reference. With
# 40 per group the reassignments are sampled, so a seed is given.
# n_resamples = 2000 keeps the example quick; a reported p-value
# deserves the default of 10,000.
vargha_delaney_A(x, y, test = "permutation", n_resamples = 2000,
                 seed = 113)
vargha_delaney_A(x, y, test = "z")

# An ordinal response is supplied as an ordered factor with its levels
# stated from lowest to highest. The unordered factors rating_1 and
# rating_2 themselves are refused, with a message that says why: their
# codes follow the alphabetical order of the labels (high, low, mid).
rating_1 <- factor(c("low", "mid", "high", "high"))
rating_2 <- factor(c("low", "low", "mid", "high"))
lev <- c("low", "mid", "high")
vargha_delaney_A(factor(rating_1, levels = lev, ordered = TRUE),
                 factor(rating_2, levels = lev, ordered = TRUE))

# Completely separated groups: the estimated variance is 0, the two
# limits equal the estimate, and a warning says that they are not a
# confidence interval. The permutation test still gives a p-value, the
# share of the 252 reassignments that are also completely separated.
separated <- vargha_delaney_A(6:10, 1:5, test = "permutation")
separated
results_sentence(separated)

}
